# Migration spec is the source of truth

Read `docs/spec-format.md` before writing a spec section or a migration script. Section ownership is in `docs/product-contract.md`.

A script may upsert a collection named in `documentModel.collections`, create an index only when that entry has `create: true`, call Grok only for a mapping with `transform: "grok"`, and install the validator in `documentModel.collections[].validation`.

Run a load only when `approval.status` is `approved` and the recomputed spec hash matches `approval.specHash`. A row that fails the Grok target shape goes to the reject file. That row is not written.

For the order-management spec the allowed collections are `customers`, `addresses`, `products`, and `orders`. The only Grok call is `orders.notes` to `notesClassification`. The script creates `type_1_priceCents_1` and `customer.customerId_1_placedAt_-1`.

Each finished test run writes `runs/<specHash>/<loggedAt>/` with that hash, the approved spec, the tests, and the results. A new hash is a new directory. That folder is a log. It is not a collection, an index, or a Grok call.

```javascript
// Outside the spec: new collection, extra Grok field, unrequested index
db.reviews.insertOne(doc)
classify(customer.fullName)
db.addresses.createIndex({ customerId: 1 })

// Inside the spec
db.orders.updateOne({ _id }, { $set: orderDoc }, { upsert: true })
classify(order.notes)
db.products.createIndex({ type: 1, priceCents: 1 }, { name: "type_1_priceCents_1" })
```
